{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014Fgp2tGjB8VCntehmMFKNY","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→### Aquarium / Planted Tank Ecosystem Simulator (Rust) — Implementation Prompt v1\n     2→\n     3→Build a deterministic Rust workspace that simulates a single freshwater planted shrimp tank as a causal ecosystem. The target species is `Neocaridina davidi`. The primary interface is a terminal UI. The MVP must be scientifically plausible, testable, and incremental to implement.\n     4→\n     5→Optimize for a complete, working v1. Do not pursue research-grade completeness. Use simplified, guild-based models that preserve the main causal relationships.\n     6→\n     7→#### 1. Deliverable\n     8→\n     9→Deliver a Cargo workspace with these crates:\n    10→\n    11→- `tank_core`: deterministic simulation engine, domain types, update systems, save/load, tests\n    12→- `tank_data`: TOML preset loading and validation\n    13→- `tank_tui`: terminal UI that consumes `TankSnapshot` and dispatches `PlayerAction`\n    14→- `tank_scenarios`: scenario fixtures used by regression tests and manual play\n    15→\n    16→The MVP must allow a user to:\n    17→\n    18→- choose a tank size and fill height\n    19→- choose substrate, source water, plants, filter, light, heater, and shrimp count\n    20→- run the simulation forward in hours/days/weeks\n    21→- feed the tank, change water, trim plants, clean the filter, siphon detritus, and change ambient temperature\n    22→- observe chemistry, plant status, algae/periphyton pressure, shrimp condition, reproduction, and event explanations\n    23→- save and load deterministically\n    24→\n    25→#### 2. Scope\n    26→\n    27→##### Mandatory v1 scope\n    28→\n    29→Implement all of the following:\n    30→\n    31→- one tank only\n    32→- one well-mixed water column compartment\n    33→- 1-hour fixed simulation tick\n    34→- layered substrate with 1 to 3 layers\n    35→- geometry-dependent volume, wall area, and surface area\n    36→- ambient temperature as a runtime player action\n    37→- water temperature dynamics with optional heater\n    38→- dissolved oxygen tracking\n    39→- nitrogen cycle: detritus -> mineralization -> TAN -> nitrite -> nitrate\n    40→- NH3 fraction derived from TAN, pH, and temperature\n    41→- minimal ions sufficient to derive GH, KH, TDS, and conductivity\n    42→- 2 plant guilds: `FastStem`, `RootFeedingRosette`\n    43→- 2 algae/biofilm pools: suspended algae and periphyton\n    44→- decomposer microbes plus nitrifier guilds: ammonia oxidizers, nitrite oxidizers, comammox\n    45→- one aggregate microfauna pool with ecological effects\n    46→- shrimp population with adults, juveniles, berried females, condition, molt stress, and reproduction readiness\n    47→- event log with machine-readable kinds and cause codes\n    48→- deterministic save/load\n    49→- terminal UI with overview, chemistry, biology, actions, and log screens\n    50→\n    51→##### Stretch only after all mandatory tests pass\n    52→\n    53→These may be added later, but are not required for v1 acceptance:\n    54→\n    55→- seasonal ambient schedules\n    56→- fertilizer dosing\n    57→- top-off and evaporation\n    58→- floating plants\n    59→- denitrification\n    60→- richer plant packs\n    61→- more detailed carbonate chemistry\n    62→\n    63→##### Out of scope for v1\n    64→\n    65→Do not implement:\n    66→\n    67→- fish\n    68→- snails\n    69→- disease-specific pathology\n    70→- saltwater chemistry\n    71→- CFD or spatial flow\n    72→- photorealistic graphics\n    73→- multiplayer\n    74→- online features\n    75→\n    76→#### 3. Modeling stance\n    77→\n    78→Use a mechanistic, guild-based model. Preserve these causal truths:\n    79→\n    80→- tank size changes dilution, thermal inertia, gas exchange, and stability\n    81→- nitrification is biological and takes time to mature\n    82→- ammonia risk depends on pH and temperature, not TAN alone\n    83→- rising temperature lowers oxygen saturation\n    84→- rooted plants benefit from nutrient-rich substrate more than inert substrate\n    85→- plants and algae compete for light and nutrients\n    86→- periphyton is both a nuisance and a shrimp food resource\n    87→- overfeeding raises detritus, oxygen demand, TAN, and algae pressure\n    88→- `Neocaridina davidi` reproduction is worse at sustained high temperature\n    89→\n    90→Use heuristics anchored by the supplied references. No new literature search is required for v1.\n    91→\n    92→#### 4. Determinism and numeric contract\n    93→\n    94→- Use a fixed 1-hour step.\n    95→- Use `f64` internally for all continuous values.\n    96→- Use an explicit RNG stored in state, for example `ChaCha8Rng`.\n    97→- Never call thread-local RNG inside `tank_core`.\n    98→- Determinism is required for the same seed, action schedule, binary, target triple, and toolchain.\n    99→- Cross-platform bitwise identity is not required.\n   100→- Save files must include full state, queued actions, RNG state, app version, and schema version.\n   101→- Invalid actions must return a typed validation error. Do not silently clamp user inputs except for tiny floating-point cleanup.\n   102→- Enforce invariants after every update:\n   103→  - no negative mass pools\n   104→  - no negative populations\n   105→  - `0.0 <= index <= 1.0` for health/maturity/stress-style values\n   106→  - temperature stays above `0.0 C` unless a scenario explicitly allows freezing\n   107→\n   108→#### 5. Units and state rules\n   109→\n   110→Use these conventions consistently:\n   111→\n   112→- store dissolved and particulate substances as total tank amounts, not concentrations\n   113→- use `_mg_total`, `_g_total`, `_meq_total`, `_count`, `_c`, `_l`, `_cm`, `_m2`\n   114→- derive all `mg/L` values from total amount divided by current water volume\n   115→- treat TDS and conductivity as derived display metrics, not hidden source-of-truth state\n   116→\n   117→Minimum tracked dissolved pools:\n   118→\n   119→- TAN as `ammonia_total_mg_n_total`\n   120→- `nitrite_mg_n_total`\n   121→- `nitrate_mg_n_total`\n   122→- `phosphate_mg_p_total`\n   123→- `dissolved_oxygen_mg_total`\n   124→- `dissolved_inorganic_carbon_mg_c_total`\n   125→- `dissolved_organic_carbon_mg_c_total`\n   126→- `dissolved_organic_nitrogen_mg_n_total`\n   127→- `alkalinity_meq_total`\n   128→- ions: Ca, Mg, Na, K, HCO3, Cl, SO4 as total mg\n   129→\n   130→Derived display values:\n   131→\n   132→- `tan_mg_l`\n   133→- `nh3_mg_l`\n   134→- `nitrite_mg_l`\n   135→- `nitrate_mg_l`\n   136→- `phosphate_mg_l`\n   137→- `do_mg_l`\n   138→- `gh_d`\n   139→- `kh_d`\n   140→- `tds_mg_l`\n   141→- `conductivity_us_cm`\n   142→- `ph`\n   143→\n   144→Use these display formulas:\n   145→\n   146→- `gh_d = ((2.497 * ca_mg_l) + (4.118 * mg_mg_l)) / 17.848`\n   147→- `kh_d = (alkalinity_meq_l * 50.0) / 17.848`\n   148→- `tds_mg_l = total_tracked_ions_mg / volume_l`\n   149→- `conductivity_us_cm = tds_mg_l / 0.65`\n   150→\n   151→#### 6. Core geometry model\n   152→\n   153→Use:\n   154→\n   155→```rust\n   156→pub struct TankGeometry {\n   157→    pub length_cm: f64,\n   158→    pub width_cm: f64,\n   159→    pub height_cm: f64,\n   160→    pub fill_height_cm: f64,\n   161→    pub glass_thickness_mm: f64,\n   162→    pub open_top: bool,\n   163→}\n   164→```\n   165→\n   166→Derive exactly:\n   167→\n   168→- `water_volume_l = length_cm * width_cm * fill_height_cm / 1000.0`\n   169→- `surface_area_cm2 = length_cm * width_cm`\n   170→- `footprint_area_cm2 = length_cm * width_cm`\n   171→- `wall_area_cm2 = 2.0 * fill_height_cm * (length_cm + width_cm)`\n   172→- `mean_depth_cm = fill_height_cm`\n   173→- `surface_area_to_volume_ratio = surface_area_cm2 / water_volume_l`\n   174→- `wall_area_to_volume_ratio = wall_area_cm2 / water_volume_l`\n   175→\n   176→`open_top = false` must reduce surface heat/gas exchange by a configurable multiplier less than `1.0`. Default that multiplier to `0.25`.\n   177→\n   178→#### 7. Time model\n   179→\n   180→- One simulation tick equals one hour.\n   181→- Every 24 hourly ticks, run a daily update layer.\n   182→- Weekly or monthly bookkeeping can be simple counters updated from daily state.\n   183→- `apply_action` queues an action for the start of the next hourly tick.\n   184→- `step_hours(n)` processes exactly `n` hourly ticks in order.\n   185→- If multiple actions are queued for the same tick, process them in insertion order.\n   186→\n   187→#### 8. Public API\n   188→\n   189→These public types are required:\n   190→\n   191→```rust\n   192→pub struct SimSeed(pub u64);\n   193→\n   194→pub enum PlayerAction {\n   195→    Feed { grams: f64 },\n   196→    WaterChangePercent { percent: f64, source_profile_id: String },\n   197→    TrimPlants { fraction: f64 },\n   198→    SiphonDetritus { fraction: f64 },\n   199→    CleanFilter { intensity: f64 },\n   200→    AddShrimp { count: u32 },\n   201→    RemoveShrimp { count: u32 },\n   202→    ChangePhotoperiod { hours: f64 },\n   203→    ChangeLightIntensity { intensity_index: f64 },\n   204→    ChangeHeaterSetpoint { setpoint_c: f64 },\n   205→    ChangeAmbientTemperature { target_c: f64 },\n   206→    ChangeAeration { enabled: bool, intensity: f64 },\n   207→}\n   208→\n   209→pub trait SimulationEngine {\n   210→    fn apply_action(&mut self, action: PlayerAction) -> Result<(), SimError>;\n   211→    fn step_hours(&mut self, hours: u32) -> Result<(), SimError>;\n   212→    fn snapshot(&self) -> TankSnapshot;\n   213→    fn full_state(&self) -> &TankState;\n   214→}\n   215→```\n   216→\n   217→Validation rules:\n   218→\n   219→- percentages and fractions must be in `0.0..=1.0` unless explicitly documented as percent values\n   220→- water change percent must be `0.0..=100.0`\n   221→- counts and grams must be non-negative\n   222→- `ChangeAmbientTemperature` is mandatory and must affect water temperature only through the heat model, not by instant water temperature assignment\n   223→\n   224→#### 9. Required simulation systems\n   225→\n   226→##### Temperature\n   227→\n   228→Use this hourly discrete model:\n   229→\n   230→- `dt_s = 3600.0`\n   231→- `heat_capacity_j_per_k = volume_l * 4186.0`\n   232→- `ua_total_w_per_k = (k_surface * surface_area_m2 * top_factor) + (k_wall * wall_area_m2)`\n   233→- `q_ambient_w = ua_total_w_per_k * (ambient_temp_c - water_temp_c)`\n   234→- `q_heater_w = max_watts * efficiency` only when heater is enabled and `water_temp_c < setpoint_c - deadband_c / 2.0`\n   235→- the heater must never cool the tank\n   236→- light heating may be ignored in v1\n   237→\n   238→Then:\n   239→\n   240→- `delta_temp_c = (q_ambient_w + q_heater_w) * dt_s / heat_capacity_j_per_k`\n   241→- `water_temp_c += delta_temp_c`\n   242→\n   243→Required outcomes:\n   244→\n   245→- smaller tanks respond faster than larger tanks\n   246→- higher ambient temperature lowers DO saturation\n   247→- if ambient exceeds the heater setpoint, heater power eventually goes to zero\n   248→\n   249→##### Nitrogen cycle\n   250→\n   251→Required pathway:\n   252→\n   253→- feed -> particulate organics -> dissolved organics -> TAN -> nitrite -> nitrate\n   254→\n   255→Implement:\n   256→\n   257→- a particulate detritus pool\n   258→- a fine detritus pool\n   259→- dissolved organic carbon and nitrogen pools\n   260→- a decomposer biomass or capacity term\n   261→- nitrifier guild biomasses for ammonia oxidizers, nitrite oxidizers, and comammox\n   262→\n   263→Use Monod-style bounded rates:\n   264→\n   265→- `rate = vmax * biomass * f_temp * f_pH * f_do * substrate / (k_substrate + substrate)`\n   266→\n   267→Rules:\n   268→\n   269→- nitrification must be biomass-limited\n   270→- nitrification must be oxygen-limited\n   271→- nitrification must be slower in immature filters\n   272→- comammox may convert TAN directly to nitrate with a lower `vmax`\n   273→- rate clamps must prevent consuming more substrate than exists\n   274→\n   275→Use `4.57 mg O2` demand per `1 mg N` nitrified to nitrate.\n   276→\n   277→##### Ammonia speciation\n   278→\n   279→Store TAN only. Derive NH3 each tick using:\n   280→\n   281→- `pKa = 0.09018 + 2729.92 / (273.2 + temp_c)`\n   282→- `fraction_nh3 = 1.0 / (1.0 + 10.0_f64.powf(pKa - ph))`\n   283→- `nh3_mg_l = tan_mg_l * fraction_nh3`\n   284→\n   285→NH3-driven stress must be stronger than TAN-driven stress.\n   286→\n   287→##### Dissolved oxygen\n   288→\n   289→Track dissolved oxygen explicitly.\n   290→\n   291→Use:\n   292→\n   293→- `do_sat_mg_l` from linear interpolation over this table:\n   294→  - `0 C -> 14.6`\n   295→  - `10 C -> 11.3`\n   296→  - `20 C -> 9.1`\n   297→  - `30 C -> 7.6`\n   298→\n   299→Then update hourly:\n   300→\n   301→- `reaeration = kLa * (do_sat_mg_l - do_mg_l)`\n   302→- `photosynthesis` from plants and periphyton during lit hours only\n   303→- `respiration` from shrimp, plants, microbes, algae, and decomposition\n   304→- `nitrification_o2_cost` from the nitrification step\n   305→\n   306→Required outcomes:\n   307→\n   308→- night oxygen dips are possible\n   309→- aeration increases gas exchange\n   310→- overfeeding can push DO down through decomposition demand\n   311→\n   312→##### pH and alkalinity\n   313→\n   314→Do not keep pH constant.\n   315→\n   316→Use a simplified approximation for v1:\n   317→\n   318→- nitrification consumes alkalinity\n   319→- photosynthesis lowers DIC during lit hours\n   320→- respiration and decomposition raise DIC\n   321→- water changes mix alkalinity and DIC from source water\n   322→\n   323→Approximate pH from alkalinity and DIC each tick with a bounded monotonic function. Use this default formula unless replaced by a documented equivalent:\n   324→\n   325→- `alkalinity_meq_l = alkalinity_meq_total / volume_l`\n   326→- `dic_mmol_l = (dissolved_inorganic_carbon_mg_c_total / 12.0) / volume_l`\n   327→- `ph = clamp(6.3 + log10(max(alkalinity_meq_l, 0.05)) - log10(max(dic_mmol_l, 0.02)), 5.5, 8.5)`\n   328→\n   329→Alkalinity consumption from nitrification must be modeled. Use `0.1428 meq` alkalinity consumed per `1 mg N` nitrified.\n   330→\n   331→##### Substrate\n   332→\n   333→Support 1 to 3 layers. Required kinds:\n   334→\n   335→- inert sand\n   336→- inert gravel\n   337→- active planted substrate\n   338→- coarse porous media mix\n   339→\n   340→Each layer must affect:\n   341→\n   342→- rooted nutrient access\n   343→- detritus trapping\n   344→- colonizable area\n   345→- low-oxygen tendency\n   346→- shrimp grazing surface proxy\n   347→\n   348→Use a substrate nutrient store for rooted plants. Active substrate must start with higher nutrient stores and higher CEC proxy than inert substrate.\n   349→\n   350→##### Plants\n   351→\n   352→Mandatory plant guilds:\n   353→\n   354→- `FastStem`\n   355→- `RootFeedingRosette`\n   356→\n   357→Daily net plant growth must depend on:\n   358→\n   359→- light\n   360→- temperature\n   361→- nitrogen availability\n   362→- phosphorus availability\n   363→- carbon availability proxy from DIC\n   364→- habitat factor\n   365→- crowding/self-shading\n   366→\n   367→Use a bounded form such as:\n   368→\n   369→- `net_growth = biomass * max_rate * f_light * f_temp * min(f_n, f_p, f_c) * habitat_factor * (1 - crowding) - respiration - senescence`\n   370→\n   371→Rules:\n   372→\n   373→- `FastStem` takes most nutrients from the water column\n   374→- `RootFeedingRosette` preferentially draws from substrate nutrient stores\n   375→- active substrate and adequate depth must improve `RootFeedingRosette` growth versus inert sand\n   376→- if plant biomass declines, a fraction must enter detritus\n   377→\n   378→##### Algae and periphyton\n   379→\n   380→Track:\n   381→\n   382→- suspended algae biomass\n   383→- periphyton biomass\n   384→\n   385→Daily algae growth must depend on:\n   386→\n   387→- light\n   388→- temperature\n   389→- dissolved nutrients\n   390→- weak plant competition\n   391→- available colonizable area for periphyton\n   392→\n   393→Periphyton must:\n   394→\n   395→- provide shrimp food\n   396→- contribute to nuisance risk\n   397→- scale with surfaces and light\n   398→- support event generation when high\n   399→\n   400→##### Microbes and microfauna\n   401→\n   402→Use simplified hidden state:\n   403→\n   404→- decomposer biomass or capacity\n   405→- nitrifier guild biomasses\n   406→- one aggregate microfauna grazing index\n   407→\n   408→Microfauna effects in v1:\n   409→\n   410→- modestly increase mineralization efficiency\n   411→- consume some periphyton/bacterial resource\n   412→- be reduced by strong shrimp grazing pressure\n   413→\n   414→Do not model species-level microfauna.\n   415→\n   416→##### Shrimp population\n   417→\n   418→Use aggregate state for:\n   419→\n   420→- adults\n   421→- juveniles\n   422→- berried females\n   423→- average condition `0..1`\n   424→- molt stress `0..1`\n   425→- reproductive readiness `0..1`\n   426→- egg progress in days\n   427→\n   428→Daily condition must depend on:\n   429→\n   430→- food and periphyton availability\n   431→- dissolved oxygen\n   432→- NH3\n   433→- nitrite\n   434→- temperature\n   435→- mineral status proxy from GH\n   436→- recent instability from large chemistry swings\n   437→\n   438→Reproduction rules:\n   439→\n   440→- use an eligible female proxy of `0.5 * adults`, minus current berried females\n   441→- `p_spawn = base_spawn_rate * f_temp * f_condition * f_stability * f_mineral`\n   442→- sample new berried females stochastically from eligible females\n   443→- egg development progresses daily\n   444→- hatch success depends on condition, oxygen, temperature, and stability\n   445→\n   446→Temperature anchor for reproduction:\n   447→\n   448→- best around `22 C` to `26 C`\n   449→- clearly worse by `30 C`\n   450→- near-zero by `33 C`\n   451→\n   452→Molt stress must rise under:\n   453→\n   454→- low GH\n   455→- abrupt water chemistry changes\n   456→- poor condition\n   457→- thermal stress\n   458→\n   459→Mortality must be probabilistic and increase with:\n   460→\n   461→- NH3\n   462→- severe nitrite\n   463→- low DO\n   464→- sustained heat stress\n   465→- severe molt stress\n   466→- starvation\n   467→\n   468→#### 10. Update order\n   469→\n   470→Use this stable hourly pipeline:\n   471→\n   472→1. apply queued actions\n   473→2. update current water volume if a water change occurred\n   474→3. mix source water chemistry and temperature for water changes\n   475→4. update water temperature from ambient and heater\n   476→5. compute light on/off state for the current hour\n   477→6. process feed inputs and excretion\n   478→7. move detritus through decomposition and mineralization\n   479→8. update nitrification and nitrifier growth/decay\n   480→9. update DIC, alkalinity, and pH\n   481→10. update dissolved oxygen\n   482→11. update hourly plant and periphyton photosynthesis/respiration effects\n   483→12. update shrimp hourly stress accumulators\n   484→13. emit hourly events and warnings\n   485→14. refresh derived observables\n   486→\n   487→After every 24 hourly ticks, run this daily pipeline:\n   488→\n   489→1. net plant biomass change\n   490→2. net algae/periphyton biomass change\n   491→3. microfauna turnover\n   492→4. shrimp condition, molt stress, reproduction, hatching, and juvenile recruitment\n   493→5. mortality checks\n   494→6. biofilter maturity summary update\n   495→7. long-term stability metrics\n   496→\n   497→#### 11. Events and observability\n   498→\n   499→Use machine-readable events.\n   500→\n   501→```rust\n   502→pub struct SimEvent {\n   503→    pub day: u32,\n   504→    pub hour: u8,\n   505→    pub severity: EventSeverity,\n   506→    pub kind: EventKind,\n   507→    pub cause_codes: Vec<EventCause>,\n   508→    pub summary: String,\n   509→}\n   510→```\n   511→\n   512→Required `EventKind` values:\n   513→\n   514→- `CycleProgressing`\n   515→- `AmmoniaWarning`\n   516→- `NitriteWarning`\n   517→- `OxygenDip`\n   518→- `AlgaeRiskRising`\n   519→- `AlgaeBloom`\n   520→- `BiofilmMaturityIncrease`\n   521→- `ShrimpBerried`\n   522→- `EggFailure`\n   523→- `MoltStressWarning`\n   524→- `FilterCleaningSetback`\n   525→- `SubstrateFoulingWarning`\n   526→- `StabilityImproving`\n   527→\n   528→Tests must assert on `kind` and `cause_codes`, not exact summary text.\n   529→\n   530→Keep at least the last `200` events in state. `TankSnapshot` must expose the last `20`.\n   531→\n   532→#### 12. Data files and saves\n   533→\n   534→Use TOML for presets.\n   535→\n   536→Required preset sets:\n   537→\n   538→- source water: `soft_acidic`, `moderate`, `hard_shrimp`, `ro_like`\n   539→- substrate: `inert_sand`, `inert_gravel`, `active_planted`, `coarse_porous`\n   540→- plants: `fast_stem`, `root_rosette`\n   541→- shrimp: `neocaridina_davidi`\n   542→- process parameters: one default pack\n   543→- scenarios: `nano_cycle`, `medium_planted`, `warm_room`\n   544→\n   545→Each preset file must contain:\n   546→\n   547→- `id`\n   548→- `name`\n   549→- parameter values\n   550→- optional `[provenance]` block with:\n   551→  - `source_title`\n   552→  - `source_year`\n   553→  - `confidence`\n   554→  - `notes`\n   555→\n   556→Per-field provenance is not required for v1.\n   557→\n   558→Save files must be JSON and contain:\n   559→\n   560→- schema version\n   561→- app version\n   562→- full `TankState`\n   563→- queued actions\n   564→- RNG state\n   565→\n   566→#### 13. TUI MVP\n   567→\n   568→Provide these screens:\n   569→\n   570→- `Overview`: day/hour, temperature, pH, DO, TAN, NH3, nitrite, nitrate, plant status, shrimp status, warnings\n   571→- `Chemistry`: chemistry details and recent trends\n   572→- `Biology`: plants, algae/periphyton, shrimp, biofilter maturity, hidden-state summaries\n   573→- `Actions`: forms for feed, water change, trim, siphon, clean filter, add/remove shrimp, change light, change heater, change ambient temperature\n   574→- `Log`: chronological event list\n   575→\n   576→Minimum controls:\n   577→\n   578→- step `1 hour`\n   579→- step `1 day`\n   580→- step `1 week`\n   581→- pause/autoadvance\n   582→- save/load\n   583→- quit\n   584→\n   585→The TUI must only use `TankSnapshot` plus action dispatch. It must not directly mutate low-level state.\n   586→\n   587→#### 14. Acceptance tests\n   588→\n   589→All of the following tests are mandatory.\n   590→\n   591→1. `determinism_same_seed`\n   592→   - same seed, same scenario, same action schedule, same binary/target\n   593→   - after `720` hours, serialized snapshots must be identical\n   594→\n   595→2. `save_load_resume`\n   596→   - run `240` hours, save, load, run `480` more hours\n   597→   - final snapshot must match an uninterrupted `720`-hour run exactly\n   598→\n   599→3. `water_change_mass_balance`\n   600→   - perform a `50%` water change using a zero-nutrient source profile\n   601→   - dissolved total masses for TAN, nitrite, nitrate, phosphate, DIC, and tracked ions must drop by `50% +/- 0.5%`\n   602→\n   603→4. `tank_size_response`\n   604→   - identical stocked scenarios in `10 L` and `100 L`\n   605→   - after the same feed pulse, peak TAN in `mg/L` in `10 L` must be at least `5x` the `100 L` peak\n   606→   - after ambient rises from `24 C` to `31 C`, the `10 L` tank must reach half of its eventual temperature delta in fewer than half as many ticks as the `100 L` tank\n   607→\n   608→5. `ambient_temperature_action`\n   609→   - start at stable `24 C`\n   610→   - change ambient to `31 C`\n   611→   - water temperature must rise gradually and not instantly jump to `31 C`\n   612→   - DO saturation target must decrease\n   613→   - heater output must be zero once water temperature is at or above setpoint\n   614→\n   615→6. `cycling_seeded_vs_unseeded`\n   616→   - identical new tanks, one seeded and one unseeded\n   617→   - the seeded tank must reach `TAN < 0.1 mg/L` and `nitrite < 0.1 mg/L` earlier\n   618→   - the unseeded tank must show non-zero TAN and nitrite peaks\n   619→\n   620→7. `overfeeding_effects`\n   621→   - compare control feeding versus repeated overfeeding for `14` days\n   622→   - overfed tank must end with higher detritus, lower nightly minimum DO, and higher algae nuisance index\n   623→\n   624→8. `rooted_substrate_advantage`\n   625→   - same `RootFeedingRosette` plant in active substrate versus inert sand\n   626→   - after `30` days, biomass in active substrate must be at least `15%` higher\n   627→\n   628→9. `thermal_reproduction_penalty`\n   629→   - identical shrimp scenarios at `25 C` and `30 C` for `60` days\n   630→   - the `30 C` tank must end with at least `20%` lower reproductive readiness and fewer juveniles\n   631→\n   632→10. `event_generation`\n   633→    - a warm, overfed, weak-aeration scenario must emit at least one of:\n   634→      - `AmmoniaWarning`\n   635→      - `OxygenDip`\n   636→      - `AlgaeRiskRising`\n   637→    - emitted events must contain non-empty `cause_codes`\n   638→\n   639→#### 15. Milestones\n   640→\n   641→Implement in this order:\n   642→\n   643→1. workspace skeleton, core types, config loading, RNG wrapper, save/load roundtrip\n   644→2. geometry, source water, water changes, temperature model, ambient temperature action\n   645→3. dissolved pools, NH3 calculation, DO model, event framework\n   646→4. detritus, decomposition, nitrifier guilds, cycling behavior\n   647→5. substrate, plants, algae/periphyton, rooted substrate advantage\n   648→6. shrimp population, reproduction, molt stress, mortality\n   649→7. TUI MVP, scenarios, full regression suite\n   650→\n   651→Do not start milestone `N+1` until milestone `N` has passing tests.\n   652→\n   653→#### 16. Definition of done\n   654→\n   655→v1 is done when:\n   656→\n   657→- all mandatory acceptance tests pass\n   658→- a user can start a preset shrimp-planted tank at multiple sizes\n   659→- the tank can cycle and stabilize over simulated weeks\n   660→- the user can feed, change water, and change ambient temperature\n   661→- chemistry and biology change in causal, inspectable ways\n   662→- shrimp can both succeed and fail reproductively depending on conditions\n   663→- save/load preserves deterministic continuation\n   664→- the TUI exposes the required views and actions"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MtcvhTKtbDxfRsSQuXRy5k","session_id":"aebe6b9c-6489-43f0-a717-82e4691947f3","uuid":"3ed8b824-421d-40eb-92c8-a0970e6469ed"}
